WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.020
Welcome to this short course,
Automated Testing for LLM Ops,

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.880
built in partnership with CircleCI.

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.640
Software testing helps
you identify bugs

4
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.980
and security vulnerabilities
in your applications,

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:17.050
and automated testing frees up your
time and energy further, so they can

6
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.560
focus on the creative parts of
designing and building

7
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.460
your application. In this course,
you learn modern

8
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.460
software engineering practices
focused on testing

9
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.360
for the practical
development and deployment of LLM-based

10
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.260
applications.

11
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.270
Two kinds of LLM
evaluations that you implement

12
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.300
in this course are rule-based
evaluations and model-graded evaluations.

13
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.700
Rule-based evals use string
or pattern matching, for

14
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.280
example, regular
expression matching, and

15
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.140
are fast and cost-effective to
run.

16
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.300
I use these whenever I
want to evaluate outputs that

17
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.460
have a clear right answer,
such as sentiment

18
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.600
classification and if, say,
you have ground-truth labels.

19
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.500
Rule-based
evals are quick and cheap to run, so

20
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.630
you can run these
tests every time you

21
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.760
commit a code change
to get fast feedback

22
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.360
on the health of
your application.

23
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.300
Model graded evaluations
are relevant for

24
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.740
applications where there
are many possible good or bad

25
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.120
outputs.

26
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.040
For example, if you ask an LLM
to write text content for you,

27
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.060
there can be more than one high-quality
response.

28
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.420
Here, you might prompt
an evaluation LLM to have it

29
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.540
assess the quality of the output
of your application LLM.

30
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.000
In other words,
use an LLM to evaluate

31
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.780
the output of another LLM.

32
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.380
Model-graded evals take more time
and cost more, but

33
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.920
they allow you to
assess more complex outputs.

34
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.940
I'm delighted to introduce our
instructor for this course, Rob

35
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.200
Zuber, Chief
Technology Officer for CircleCI.

36
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.820
Rob has spent
decades leading engineering

37
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.320
teams and also
helping customers scale

38
00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.820
up their software
delivery practice by

39
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.320
making processes repeatable,
scalable, and reliable.

40
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.800
He'll show you how to do this for your
applications as well with

41
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.140
an emphasis on testing.

42
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.080
Thanks, Andrew.

43
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.190
In your software development process,
you and your teammates may commit

44
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.940
code updates or bug fixes multiple
times per day.

45
00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.760
In this course, you'll learn to set
triggers that automatically

46
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.670
run your evaluations
whenever you or

47
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.580
your teammates commit
code changes to

48
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.220
the repository.

49
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.960
Your team may also
release updated versions of

50
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.280
the app on a broader cadence,
perhaps once every two weeks.

51
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.740
Before deploying to users,
you can also automate more holistic,

52
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.700
comprehensive, pre-release
evaluations.

53
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.000
For per-commit evals, you
can include rules-based evaluations

54
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.440
because they're fast and
cheap to run.

55
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:46.890
And for those pre-release evals, you
can include rules-based evaluations

56
00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.300
because they're fast and
cheap to run. And for those

57
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.870
pre-release evals, it may be very
helpful to use model-graded evals to do more

58
00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.180
thorough testing before deployment.

59
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.960
By the end of the course,
you will combine per-commit and pre-release evals

60
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.640
into an automated
testing suite.

61
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.840
And for this course, you'll
design tests to detect

62
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.200
hallucinations in LLM responses.

63
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.940
Many people have worked
to make this course possible. I'd

64
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.980
like to thank on the CircleCI side, Michael
Webster, Jacob Schmidt, and

65
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:16.460
Emma Webb.

66
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.580
From DeepLearning.ai, Eddie Hsu and Eshmal
Gargari have also

67
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.180
contributed to this course.

68
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.350
The first lesson will
be a quick overview

69
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.910
of continuous integration terms
and technologies that we'll

70
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.480
use as the foundation
for building our automated

71
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.440
LLM testing pipeline.

72
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.840
When you finish this course,
that will be a

73
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.000
real testament to your
dedication to building good

74
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:39.680
applications.

75
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.940
Or if you're not sure how much you'll use
these ideas, you can

76
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.480
still test the waters.

77
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.960
So let's go on to the next
video to get started.
